# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is git-ignored.

# --- Session ---------------------------------------------------------------
# NextAuth signing secret. Generate one with:  npx auth secret
AUTH_SECRET=""

# --- Where your days start and end -----------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. An IANA zone name; defaults to Europe/Berlin.
#
# Follow-up dates are plain days with no time in them, and "is this due?" is
# answered on the server while the buttons that set it run in your browser. On
# Vercel the server is UTC, so without a stated zone those two disagree between
# midnight and 02:00 German time — a follow-up set for tomorrow shows as due
# tonight. This is the one place that disagreement is settled.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser is one of the two halves that must agree. It
# is a preference, not a credential; nothing secret is exposed by it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_OPERATOR_TIME_ZONE="Europe/Berlin"

# --- The nightly refresh ----------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret for the scheduled refresh at /api/cron/refresh, which has no
# browser and therefore no session. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# On Vercel, set this in the project's environment variables and the platform
# sends it as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` on every cron invocation.
#
# Leave it unset and the cron route refuses every caller — deliberately. That
# route spends money against the Places API, so an unconfigured deployment must
# fail closed rather than stand open.
CRON_SECRET=""

# --- The one account -------------------------------------------------------
# There is no sign-up. These two values are the account.
# Generate the hash with:  node scripts/hash-password.mjs 'your passphrase'
LEAD_ENGINE_OWNER_EMAIL="you@example.com"
LEAD_ENGINE_OWNER_PASSWORD_HASH="scrypt:...:..."

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_ — the browser never talks to Supabase.
# Supabase → Project Settings → API
SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project-ref.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY="sb_secret_..."

# --- Discovery -------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-only. Google Cloud console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials.
# Enable BOTH "Places API (New)" and "Geocoding API" on the key:
#   Places API (New)  discovery and enrichment
#   Geocoding API     resolving a typed location so the radius filter works
#
# Restrict the key to those two APIs. It is never sent to the browser, so an IP
# or referrer restriction is not what protects it — the API restriction is.
#
# Cost note: the search field mask asks for websiteUri, rating and
# userRatingCount, which are Enterprise-tier fields, so discovery bills at Text
# Search Enterprise ($35/1000, first 1,000 calls each month free). One call
# returns up to 20 businesses. See lib/providers/google-places/skus.ts.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=""

# --- Enrichment ------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. PageSpeed Insights scores every saved lead's website on mobile, and
# the API answers without a key — just on a much tighter quota shared by
# everything on your IP, which in practice means 429s during a big re-audit.
#
# With a key the quota is yours: 25,000 requests a day, free. Same Google Cloud
# project as above; enable "PageSpeed Insights API" and create a SECOND key
# restricted to it. Deliberately not the Places key — that one is restricted to
# two APIs on purpose, and widening it to a third would undo the restriction
# that is the only thing protecting it.
#
# Leave this blank and enrichment still works. Audits whose PageSpeed call is
# rate-limited say so on the lead rather than failing.
GOOGLE_PAGESPEED_API_KEY=""

# --- The call assistant ----------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Which implementation answers the three assistant interfaces —
# the briefing before a call, the tips during it, the summary after it.
#
#   mock       (default) answers from the fixtures in lib/assistant/fixtures/,
#              with 300-800ms of artificial delay so the call surface is built
#              against real latency. Costs nothing and needs no key. This is
#              also the test environment: the three demo scripts run against it.
#   anthropic  real models, one per job — Sonnet 5 writes the briefing and the
#              summary, Haiku 4.5 answers a mid-call tip. Needs the key below
#              AND a non-zero assistant_ceiling_usd; without either it refuses
#              every request and says which one is missing.
#
# Leave it unset unless you mean it. The default must not be the path to a
# billable call — the same posture CRON_SECRET takes, for the same reason.
ASSISTANT_PROVIDER="mock"

# Server-only, and there is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ form. The three
# surfaces that reach a model all go through route handlers behind lib/api/
# guard.ts; a key in the client bundle is a key on a stranger's laptop.
#
# Required when ASSISTANT_PROVIDER is "anthropic" and ignored otherwise. It is
# read at the moment of a request rather than captured at startup, so rotating
# it does not need a deploy.
#
# COST. Tokens, in two directions, at two prices, with no free tier of any kind
# — which is the whole reason there is a second ceiling. Sonnet 5 is $3/$10 per
# million in/out ($2/$10 through 2026-08-31), Haiku 4.5 is $1/$5. In practice a
# prepared call is a couple of cents and a mid-call tip is a fraction of one.
# Every request is written to api_usage with its own SKU, so the running total
# on the search bar is the whole bill and not the Google part of it.
#
# THE CEILING IS NOT HERE. It is app_settings.assistant_ceiling_usd, it defaults
# to 0, and 0 refuses every model call. Raise it beside the running total, in
# the app. See lib/assistant/anthropic/spend.ts for why it is a second number
# rather than a share of monthly_ceiling_usd.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# --- Reviews for the briefing ----------------------------------------------
# No second key: reviews use GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY above.
#
# They are a SEPARATE Places request on purpose. `reviews` is an Enterprise +
# Atmosphere field and Places bills a request at the most expensive field in its
# mask, so folding it into the details mask would have raised the price of every
# save and every nightly refresh across the whole book — to buy reviews for the
# hundreds of leads nobody ever rings. Instead it is one request per prepared
# call ($25/1000, first 1,000 each month free), cached 30 days in place_reviews,
# and refused by the same ceiling as everything else.
#
# See lib/providers/google-places/reviews.ts.

# --- The recogniser during a call ------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Which implementation turns speech into transcript rows. The one
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable in this project, and it has to be: the recogniser runs
# in the browser, so the choice of recogniser has to be readable there. It is
# not a credential — the answer is "mock" or "browser".
#
#   mock     (default) plays the fixture call in lib/transcript/fixtures/ back
#            on a timer, interim results and all. No microphone is opened and
#            no audio leaves the machine. This is how the whole chain gets
#            tested — surface, batching, routes, rows — reproducibly.
#   browser  Chrome's own SpeechRecognition, lang de-DE, continuous. Free: no
#            key, no account, nothing that lands on a bill.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS THE MOCK, and the reason is not cost. Chrome does not
# recognise speech on the device — it sends the audio to Google — so an
# unconfigured deployment must not be one keystroke away from streaming a
# stranger's voice off the machine because somebody opened a page. Setting this
# is the act of deciding to. The surface says which one is behind the button and
# prints the provider's own disclosure above it.
#
# There is also no speaker separation and there is not going to be: one
# microphone on a speakerphone hears one room, so every row is written
# speaker='unknown' rather than with a guess.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TRANSCRIPT_PROVIDER="mock"

# --- The MCP endpoint -------------------------------------------------------
# Bearer token for /api/mcp, which exposes Lead Engine as two tools an
# assistant can call: search_places and save_leads. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Point a client at it with:
#   { "url": "https://<your-deployment>/api/mcp",
#     "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer $MCP_TOKEN" } }
#
# Leave it unset and the route refuses every caller, the same way the cron route
# does and for a stronger reason. There is no session behind this door — an MCP
# client is a program with no cookie — so this token is the whole of the lock,
# and what it stands in front of is a tool that spends money against Google,
# writes rows into the permanent leads library, and fetches other people's
# websites from this server's address, all without anybody watching. An unset
# variable must never become an open endpoint that does any of that.
#
# Rotating it costs nothing: change it here and in the client. Nothing is
# derived from it and no stored data depends on it.
MCP_TOKEN=""
